## Solution

**Identify the group.** $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{F}_5) \cong A_5$, the alternating group on 5 letters, with order $|A_5| = 60 = 2^2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5$.

**Admissible orders.** An admissible order is a divisor $m$ of $|G|$ with $\gcd(m, |G|/m) = 1$. For $|G| = 2^2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5$, these are obtained by choosing, for each prime, either the full prime power or nothing:

$$m \in \{1,\; 4,\; 3,\; 5,\; 12,\; 20,\; 15,\; 60\}.$$

That is 8 admissible orders (including the trivial $m=1$ and $m=60$).

**Check Hall subgroup existence.** Since the coprimality condition is already built into "admissible," a Hall subgroup of admissible order $m$ exists iff a subgroup of order $m$ exists.

| $m$ | Subgroup of order $m$ in $A_5$? | Reason |
|-----|------|--------|
| 1 | Yes | trivial subgroup |
| 60 | Yes | $A_5$ itself |
| 4 | Yes | Sylow 2-subgroups ($V_4$), order 4 |
| 3 | Yes | Sylow 3-subgroups (3-cycles) |
| 5 | Yes | Sylow 5-subgroups (5-cycles) |
| 12 | Yes | Point stabilizer $\cong A_4$, order 12 |
| 20 | **No** | see below |
| 15 | **No** | see below |

**$m = 20$ fails.** A subgroup of order 20 has index 3, yielding a nontrivial homomorphism $\varphi: A_5 \to S_3$. Since $A_5$ is simple, $\ker\varphi$ is either $1$ or $A_5$. If $\ker\varphi = 1$, then $A_5$ embeds into $S_3$, impossible ($60 > 6$). If $\ker\varphi = A_5$, the image is trivial, contradicting index 3. So no subgroup of order 20 exists.

**$m = 15$ fails.** Any group of order $15 = 3\cdot 5$ is cyclic (since $3 \nmid (5-1)=4$, the Sylow subgroups are unique and the group is $\mathbb{Z}_{15}$). This requires an element of order 15 in $A_5$. The element orders in $A_5$ are $\{1, 2, 3, 5\}$ (identity, double transpositions, 3-cycles, 5-cycles), so no element of order 15 exists. Hence no subgroup of order 15.

**Conclusion.** Among the 8 admissible orders, $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{F}_5) \cong A_5$ fails to have a Hall subgroup for exactly $m \in \{15, 20\}$.

The number of distinct admissible orders for which $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{F}_5)$ does not have a Hall subgroup is $\boxed{2}$.

